# `install` (Docs/install-plan.html): from the install medium onto an empty
# SATA disk, then the installed system by itself, an upgrade from a newer
# medium (new kernel on trial in the spare slot), boot and os again with the
# data kept, and last everything erased.
@sata
@blank-disk 96
@install-medium
$ install
~ ^This install medium carries BeeCodersOs b\d+\.$
~ ^  sata0 +96 MiB  "QEMU HARDDISK": empty$
~ ^ +install sata0  would install$
Nothing was changed.
@disk-untouched

# Anything but "yes" changes nothing.
$& install sata0
> no
$wait
~ ^  after: BeeCodersOs b\d+: boot 16 MiB, os 39 MiB, data 40 MiB$
Nothing was changed.
@disk-untouched

$& install sata0
> yes
$wait
  clearing the partition table
  writing the boot area (bootloader and both kernel slots)
  making the os file system
  making the data file system
  checking what was written
  writing the partition table
Installed on sata0. Take out the install medium and type: reboot

# The installed system, without the medium: both file systems, A/B slots.
@boot-disk
~ ^\[slots\] slot a \(good slot: a\)$
~ SATA port 0 "QEMU HARDDISK", 96 MiB, partition: BeeFS mounted \(boot #1\)$
~ ^\[disk\] data partition, 40 MiB: mounted at /data$
$ pwd
/data
$ cat welcome.txt
~ ^Your files live here
$ slots
booted slot: a
good slot:   a
$ echo mine > notes.txt
$ mkdir /data/work
$ echo deep > work/a.txt
$ echo a setting > /system/setting
$ ls /
~ data/
~ system/
$ df
~ ^disk: \d+ KiB used
~ ^data \(/data\): \d+ KiB used
# Copies are made piece by piece, within the data partition and across to the system's.
$ cp /data/notes.txt /data/copy.txt
$ cat /data/copy.txt
~ .
$ cp /data/notes.txt /copy-on-os.txt
$ cat /copy-on-os.txt
~ .
$ rm /data/copy.txt
$ rm /copy-on-os.txt
$ mv /data/notes.txt /notes-on-os.txt
$ cat /notes-on-os.txt
mine
$ mv /notes-on-os.txt /data/notes.txt
$ rm /data
directory not empty
$ ls /data
~ notes.txt
$ install sata0
~ not started from an install medium
@reboot
$ cat /data/notes.txt
mine

# A newer medium: the same command upgrades. The disk keeps everything.
@boot-medium newer
~ ^\[disk\] data partition, 40 MiB: mounted at /data$
$ install
~ ^  sata0 +96 MiB  "QEMU HARDDISK": BeeCodersOs b\d+ \(boot, os, data\)$
~ would upgrade
$& install sata0
> yes
$wait
~ ^  after: the same, with kernel b\d+ on trial in the spare slot
~ ^  writing the new kernel \(b\d+\) into slot b$
  checking what was written
  putting slot b on trial
~ ^Kernel b\d+ is in slot b of sata0, on trial at the next start from the disk\.
$& install sata0
> yes
$wait
~ a kernel is still on trial on this disk

@boot-disk
~ ^\[slots\] trying slot b \(b\d+\): rollback armed, slot a boots next unless this one passes its health check$
~ ^\[slots\] slot b passed its health check: it is now the good slot$
$ slots
booted slot: b
good slot:   b
$ cat /data/notes.txt
mine
$ cat /data/work/a.txt
deep
$ cat /system/setting
a setting

# Boot and os again: the settings go, the files stay.
@boot-medium
$& install sata0 --reinstall
> yes
$wait
  writing the boot area (bootloader and both kernel slots)
  making the os file system again
~ ^Boot and os written again on sata0; data kept\.
@boot-disk
~ ^\[slots\] slot a \(good slot: a\)$
$ cat /data/notes.txt
mine
$ cat /system/setting
~ no such file

# Everything new takes --erase and a sentence naming the disk.
@boot-medium
$& install sata0 --erase
> erase the disk
$wait
EVERYTHING on this disk will be erased. There is no undo.
~ ^To go on, type exactly:  erase 96 MiB QEMU HARDDISK$
That is not the sentence: nothing was changed.
$ cat /data/notes.txt
mine
$& install sata0 --erase
> Erase  96 mib qemu harddisk
$wait
Installed on sata0. Take out the install medium and type: reboot
@boot-disk
$ ls /data
~ welcome.txt
!~ notes.txt
